  "id": "Compound::DB00373 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Timolol, a beta-adrenergic antagonist, is implicated in the treatment of hypertension through its ability to block adrenergic receptors, which helps lower blood pressure. This mechanism is supported by multiple pathways, as Timolol is linked to both arterial insufficiency and cold hands and feet, which are conditions that can be treated by metoprolol or propranolol, both of which are beta-adrenergic antagonists. The shared ancestry of Timolol with these drugs, as well as their common classification as organic cyclic compounds, suggests a broader pharmacological relationship within the class of beta-blockers. The evidence indicates that Timolol may exert its therapeutic effects on hypertension through a mechanism involving the modulation of adrenergic signaling, potentially through the inhibition of receptor activation and subsequent physiological responses. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of beta-adrenergic antagonism in the treatment of hypertension, with Timolol serving as a relevant agent in this context.",
